;; craft.sb: macOS sandbox profile used by bin/craft-sandbox (craft-claude-kit) to fence one Craft
;; CLI that has no confinement flag of its own.
;;
;; Parameters, passed by craft-sandbox with `sandbox-exec -D`; all are real paths (symlinks resolved):
;;   ROOT  the one folder the program may read and write (that app's workspace folder)
;;   HOME  the user's home folder
;;   BIN   the program file itself
;;   BINDIR the folder that holds the program file (read-only listing, see rule 2)
;;
;; Design: start from (allow default) and subtract, instead of (deny default) and add. The Craft
;; CLIs are large programs that load many system frameworks, fonts and caches outside the home
;; folder; an allow-list profile would break with every upstream release. So this profile removes
;; exactly four things a confined document tool should not have: the network, the user's own
;; files, write access to the rest of the disk, and Mach service lookups (which is how a program
;; asks macOS to start another program outside the sandbox).
;;
;; Rules are evaluated last-match-wins: an allow placed after a deny carves out an exception.
;;
;; Limits, stated plainly:
;;  - Everything outside HOME and /Volumes stays readable (system files, /Applications, /opt, /usr).
;;  - /private/tmp and /private/var/folders stay writable. They are shared with every other process,
;;    including the other sandboxed Craft apps, so do not treat them as private.
;;  - Mach services are denied (rule 4) except Metal's shader compiler (rule 4a); Apple Events and
;;    the pasteboard were not tested separately.
;;  - Signals are NOT confined: the program can signal (stop, kill) other processes of the same user
;;    (tested 2026-10-14). (deny signal) + (allow signal (target self)) blocked that and passed 7 of the 8
;;    smoke tests; it is not used because it was never checked against LightCraft.
;;  - sandbox-exec is deprecated by Apple but still works on current macOS.
(version 1)
(allow default)

;; 1. No network at all, including local (unix-domain) sockets. The MCP server talks to Claude Code
;;    over stdin/stdout, which is not network. This also disables the apps' own --connect/bridge
;;    modes, which this kit does not use.
(deny network*)

;; 2. Reads: nothing under the home folder or under /Volumes (external drives, network shares),
;;    except ROOT and the program file. Reading the metadata of HOME itself (not its contents) stays
;;    allowed because path lookups and some libraries stat() it.
(deny file-read* (subpath (param "HOME")) (subpath "/Volumes"))
(allow file-read* (subpath (param "ROOT")) (literal (param "BIN")))
(allow file-read-metadata (literal (param "HOME")))
;; The program's own folder (a single directory entry, not its subfolders): GPU-using apps such as
;; LightCraft look next to their executable while Metal starts, and crash with SIGSEGV when that is
;; denied (verified on LightCraft 0.8.0, macOS 26). This reveals only the file names in that
;; folder, not their contents.
(allow file-read* (literal (param "BINDIR")))

;; 3. Writes: only ROOT, the temp folders (macOS frameworks keep per-user caches and temp files in
;;    /private/var/folders and fail without them) and the device nodes a command-line program
;;    writes to: /dev/null, its terminal, and its own file descriptors. Everything else on disk is
;;    read-only to the process.
(deny file-write*
  (require-not
    (require-any
      (subpath (param "ROOT"))
      (subpath "/private/tmp")
      (subpath "/private/var/folders")
      (literal "/dev/null")
      (literal "/dev/tty")
      (regex #"^/dev/ttys[0-9]+$")
      (regex #"^/dev/fd/[0-9]+$"))))

;; 4. Mach services. macOS starts a program requested through LaunchServices (`open`, an app
;;    launch) from launchd, not as a child of this process, so that program would not inherit the
;;    rules above: it could read the home folder and use the network. Denying Mach lookups closes
;;    that route (tested: `open` of an app bundle then fails with kLSNoExecutableErr and nothing
;;    starts). Narrower denies (launchservicesd, lsd.*, coreservicesd) were tried and did not stop
;;    the launch.
(deny mach-lookup)
;; 4a. One exception: Metal's shader compiler. GPU apps (LightCraft) look up the XPC service
;;     com.apple.MTLCompilerService the first time they render; without it Metal aborts the process
;;     (exit -6 on LightCraft 0.8.0, macOS 26). It is an Apple XPC service inside Metal.framework
;;     that compiles shaders and runs in its own Apple sandbox; it cannot be asked to start other
;;     programs, so the LaunchServices route above stays closed (the doctor's `open` probe checks
;;     that). With this exception all 8 sandboxed Craft CLIs pass their smoke tests (2026-10-15).
(allow mach-lookup (xpc-service-name "com.apple.MTLCompilerService"))
